# Compiler architecture quick reference

### `SymbolTable.matchPhrase`

- Anchors on the **first significant word** of the invocation.
- Scores candidates by literal-keyword overlap.
- Calls `extractArgs` → `ExpressionParser` on each argument slot.
- `stripPatternSlop` removes leading articles (`a/an/the`) and leading kind
  words (e.g. `reason`) from extracted argument text.
- Phrase-pattern parsing uses `EnglishLexicon.findEarliestArticle(_:)`, which
  finds the **earliest** parameter article occurrence (`a`/`an` by default), not
  the last. This is a correctness invariant — do not change it.

### `ASTToIR.lowerPhraseInvocation`

1. If the invocation starts with `"invoke "` → `buildInvokeExpr` → `InvokeIR`.
2. If `matchPhrase` returns a result with `workflowStructName != nil` → emit
   `InvokeIR(toolID: "workflow:{StructName}", arguments: ordered by pattern)`.
3. If `matchPhrase` returns a regular phrase → `inlinePhrase` → `lowerBlock`
   (recursion depth-limited to 8).
4. Otherwise → `BindIR(name: "_unresolved")` placeholder.

**Argument substitution order:** sort by descending key length, then use
`WholeWordRegex.replace` (regex `\b…\b`, case-insensitive) for each substitution.
This prevents shorter keys from mangling longer ones.

### `ASTToIR.exprToText`

Do **not** prefix bare identifiers with `"the "`. This was reverted after
causing `state.get("the order.id")` bugs. Identifiers go through as-is.

### `ExpressionParser.parseAtom`

Recognises possessive `'s` chains **without** a leading article (then
delegates to `PossessiveChainParser`). The check is:
```swift
t.contains("'s ") || t.hasSuffix("'s")
```
This was added so `"order's id"` parses as a property access after text
substitution strips the leading article.

### Per-production expression grammar (Wave 5)

`ExpressionParser` (`Sources/MeridianCore/Parser/Lexical/ExpressionParser.swift`)
is a **thin orchestrator only** — it holds `symbols`/`trace`/`lexicon`/`screen`
and dispatches to one independent unit per `GrammarTable` production, each in
its own file under `Sources/MeridianCore/Parser/Lexical/Productions/`. Each
production is either a real `pegex` grammar (`*Grammar.swift`) or a dedicated
hand-written Swift function (`*Parser.swift`), decided per-production in
`GrammarProduction.implementation` — see `IMPLEMENTATION_LOG.md`'s
"Per-production expression grammar" entry for the full rationale table. There
is one expression parser implementation; do not add mode switches around
expression parsing.

- **Dispatch is nested, not flat** — this is the one structural detail that
  matters for combined expressions. `parse(_:)` is the `boolean` production;
  its atom, tried at *every* leaf position (top of the expression, an
  `either`'s body, each and/or operand), is `parseClause`: `quantifier` →
  `emptiness`/`temporal-window` → `active-verb` → `comparison` → `parseAtom`.
  `parseAtom` tries `literal-atom` → `table-lookup` → (with symbols)
  `aggregate` → `superlative` → `scalar-navigation` → `description` →
  undeclared-passive-verb error → `possessive-chain` → bare identifier. A bare
  quantifier/description/etc. with no `and`/`or` in it still flows through the
  same `parseClause` step as a combined expression's operands — never add a
  separate top-level branch for the "uncombined" case.
- New surface forms must be registered in `Sources/MeridianCore/Grammar/GrammarTable.swift`
  via `PegexSurfaceGrammar` before they are parsed ad hoc. The table is the
  source for production docs, screening anchors, and the golden-corpus coverage.
- `NonterminalScreen` is a **live pre-filter** wired into `parseClause`/
  `parseAtom` (not just benchmarking) for `quantifier`/`comparison`/
  `aggregate`/`superlative` — their anchor vocabularies are closed,
  lexicon-derived sets that fully cover what the production matches on.
  **Correctness invariant:** `NonterminalScreen`'s bitmask vocabulary has a
  fixed capacity (`Bitset128`, 128 slots); a production is only screened
  (given a nonzero mask) if *every one* of its anchors got a vocabulary slot.
  If any single anchor was dropped for capacity reasons, the whole production
  must fall back to mask `0` (always tried) — a partial mask silently
  under-represents real trigger words and wrongly screens sentences out (this
  broke `quantifier`'s `every`/`any`/`no`/… determiners during the Wave 5
  per-production rewrite; see `IMPLEMENTATION_LOG.md`). Do not screen a
  production whose anchor list can't be fully proven present in the vocabulary.
- `NonterminalScreen` anchor matching must avoid substring false positives for
  short word anchors (`in` must not match inside another word).
- `SentenceBreaker` owns statement terminator stripping; do not reintroduce
  direct `hasSuffix(".")` / `dropLast()` statement logic at call sites.

### `StatementParser.collectMultiLineCounted`

Returns `(text: String, consumed: Int)`. The `consumed` count tells
`parseBlock` how many additional lines to skip. Always use this (not
`collectMultiLine`) when iterating with an explicit index `i`.

### Multi-line phrase headers

Both `MerConfigParser` and `MeridianParser` call `collectHeaderLines(…)` when
a header line ends with `,` or other non-`:` characters. It keeps appending
continuation lines (at a deeper indent) until one ends with `:`.

### Workflow stub registration

Before lowering, `ASTToIR.lower(_ file:)` calls
`symbols.registerWorkflowPhrase(workflow:)` for every `WorkflowAST`. This
makes all workflows resolvable as phrase invocations within other workflows
(and themselves), enabling self-recursion.

### `matchPhrase`'s implicit-fill retry must not self-match the lowering workflow

`SymbolTable.matchPhrase`/`matchPhraseDirect` take an `excludingStructName:
String?` parameter. `ASTToIR.lowerWorkflow` passes its own `owningStructName`
through `defaultParam` down the block/statement lowering chain specifically so
the **low-confidence single-parameter implicit-fill retry** (not the primary,
fully-literal match) can exclude the currently-lowering workflow from its own
candidate set. Without this, a leftover non-lowerable narrative line inside a
single-parameter workflow can spuriously self-match via manufactured literal
overlap (the retry's appended connector + the workflow's own parameter kind),
silently emitting a nonsense recursive self-invocation instead of the correct
`MER2001 unresolved phrase` diagnostic. An explicit, fully-literal
self-recursive call is unaffected — it still resolves via the primary match,
which this exclusion does not touch.

### Rule/statement/prose classification precedence in executable sections

`SkillSectionBuilder.build` classifies each one-line, non-table/checklist/
code-block statement group inside an **executable** section
(`procedure`/`invariants`/`prohibitions`) in a fixed order — this is a
correctness invariant, not a heuristic to tune per-corpus:

1. Inside the guidance body of a `use judgment to follow the … guidance:`
   step → guidance prose for that step (never a rule or a statement).
2. Otherwise, if `RuleAnalyzer.classify(RuleAST(text:))` returns non-nil (a
   real Phase-8 shape: `must not` / `may` [+ bounded `up to N`] / `must be …
   by … before` / `When …, …`) → extracted into `MeridianFile.rules`, **not**
   left in the section body. `RuleAnalyzer.classify` is the only detector —
   never add an ad hoc word-list check (e.g. `hasPrefix("a ")`) at a call
   site. `applicability`/`negative-applicability` sections are exempt (their
   bullets are dispatch-phrase fragments, always mined as dispatch
   predicates).
3. Otherwise → ordinary statement, lowered per the section's role.

Rules fire identically whether the source is a flat heading-less `.meridian`
file or a `##`/`###` executable section — the old `!hasHeadings` gate that
made sectioned documents rule-inert everywhere is gone (Phase 0B, 2026-07).
Prose opt-out is **markers/comments only**: `(( role: reference ))`,
`(( inert ))`, and `#`/`>` comment lines — never inferred from content. AI-
routed steps (`use judgment to …:`, `with discretion`, `with autonomy`,
`ai-discretion`/`ai-autonomy` markers) are executable, not a prose opt-out.
Full precedence + examples: `docs/03_LANGUAGE.md` § "gbrain
SKILL surface"; migrator-side classification of the same source text during
`SKILL.md` porting is a separate policy — see `AGENTS.md` §6 pitfall #15 and
`docs/09_SKILL_PORTING.md`.

### `SkillSectionRole.reference` — the non-executable role `(( inert ))` maps to

`SkillSectionRole` (`Sources/MeridianCore/Rulebook/Rulebook.swift`) has a
`.reference` case — the role `(( role: reference ))` resolves to, and what
`(( inert ))`/`(( inert, role: … ))` are synonyms of via
`SectionRoleResolver.decide`. It is a **non-executable** role (`executes ==
false`), so both of the section-role model's two hand-written, non-`default`
exhaustive switches must carry it in their non-executable arm:
`SkillSectionRole.isExecutable` (`Rulebook.swift`) and `SkillSectionBuilder
.build`'s `switch role` (`Sources/MeridianCore/Parser/Skill/
SkillSectionBuilder.swift`, the `.template, .domain, .tools, .conventionRef,
.reference, .inert` arm). Adding any future non-executable role must extend
both switches — the compiler will refuse to build otherwise, which is the
point: there is no `default:` escape hatch on either switch. Migrator-side
classification logic (which role to *assign*) lives in
`Sources/MeridianCore/Migration/SkillMigrator+{SplitPlan,Marking}.swift`, out
of this file's glob — see `docs/09_SKILL_PORTING.md` for that policy.

